{
  "content": {
    "body": "A **finding** pebble is a DATED, APPEND-ONLY evidence report — a problem OR\na pass proof — attached to the spec/code it concerns. It is NEVER edited or\ndeleted: a new answer SUPERSEDES the old, and the LATEST entry in a\nsupersede chain is the current truth. Findings are not a component of a\nmodel or structure; those pebbles REFERENCE them (their \"findings\nreferences\"). This doc DEFINES every component and WHAT belongs under it;\nextraction / verification / fix skills REFERENCE it and own the METHOD.\n\nRead each entry as: the component, then the values/shape considered part\nof it.\n\n## Finding content — the components\n\n- **severity** — how bad it is. Under it: ONE of — critical · high · medium\n  · low · info. A pass / informational finding is typically `info`; a real\n  problem picks critical…low by blast radius.\n- **category** — what KIND of finding it is. Under it: ONE of — bug ·\n  verified-working · spec-violation · security · performance · regression ·\n  tech-debt · ux · data · other (the CLI also accepts the research-log\n  categories research · decision · assumption · measurement · incomplete).\n  `verified-working` is a PASS PROOF — green evidence that something is\n  correct, NOT a problem; this is how one finding type holds both bug\n  reports and pass evidence. The category drives the default disposition.\n- **state** (disposition) — its standing at BIRTH, immutable. Under it: ONE\n  of (new findings) — open (needs action as of the report date) · resolved\n  (settled / fixed) · info (no action implied). Immutable because findings\n  are APPEND-ONLY — you SUPERSEDE, never edit. The default is DERIVED from\n  the category (verified-working / decision → resolved; research /\n  assumption / measurement / incomplete → info; everything else → open) and\n  can be overridden. (Legacy dispositions investigating / wont-fix /\n  false-positive still load on old files.)\n- **status** — DERIVED, never authored. Under it: nothing you set — the\n  pebble's status is COMPUTED from the disposition (open → broken / red ·\n  resolved → verified / green · info → neutral) so the finding colors\n  correctly everywhere it is referenced. Set the disposition; the status\n  follows.\n- **body** — the narrative, in markdown. Under it, these SECTIONS (a\n  convention — the author owns the structure — carry the ones that apply):\n  - **## Observation** — WHAT was found and why it matters; plain-language\n    first, then the technical detail.\n  - **## Evidence** — the PROOF: file:line references, log excerpts,\n    reproduction steps, test results.\n  - **## Impact** — WHY it matters: blast radius, who / what is affected,\n    the severity rationale.\n  - **## Root cause** — the underlying cause, once analyzed.\n  - **## Solution** — what to do about it.\n  - **## Solution steps** — concrete, ordered steps to carry out the\n    solution.\n  For a PASS (verified-working), Observation + Evidence carry it; the later\n  sections may be empty.\n\n### Default body format — START from it, do NOT restructure it\n\nA new finding SEEDS with these defaults — `severity: \"medium\"`,\n`category: \"bug\"`, `state: \"open\"` — and this EXACT body skeleton. Keep the\nsix `##` headers\nverbatim and IN ORDER; fill the prose sections in place of the `<!-- … -->`\nplaceholders; APPEND bullet items under **Evidence** and numbered items\nunder **Solution steps**. Do not rename, reorder, add, or drop sections —\nthat is how the format stays machine-readable and diffable across supersedes.\n\n    ## Observation\n\n    <!-- What was found and why it matters — plain-language first, then technical detail -->\n\n    ## Evidence\n\n    - <!-- file:line references, log excerpts, reproduction steps -->\n\n    ## Impact\n\n    <!-- Why it matters — blast radius, who's affected -->\n\n    ## Root cause\n\n    <!-- The underlying cause, once analyzed -->\n\n    ## Solution\n\n    <!-- What to do about it -->\n\n    ## Solution steps\n\n    1. <!-- concrete step -->\n\n## Pebble-level components\n\n- **name** — a one-line title summarizing the finding (\"customer_id missing\n  FK constraint\", \"email uniqueness verified\").\n- **status** — derived from the disposition (see above).\n- **references** — what the finding is ABOUT. Under it: the exact\n  pebble/unit it concerns — a model field, a workflow node, an api, a\n  structure field, code, a checklist item — captured as graph edges in both\n  directions. A finding does NOT reference rules, skills, knowledge, or\n  other findings from the picker.\n- **resources** — the finding's ENTRY POINTS into the codebase (code\n  links). PURPOSE: a resource tells the AI the EXACT file — optionally a\n  symbol and line — to open FIRST when it needs to re-check or fix this\n  finding: the file(s) where the problem (or the verified behavior) lives,\n  the same spots the Evidence section cites — so it starts from the real\n  code, not re-discovery. When the AI locates the file a finding is about,\n  it SHOULD link it here. Find it with the code map (`map-search` /\n  `map-file`) — the user does the same via the app's Mapping feature — then\n  bind with `link-code` (remove with `unlink-code`). Under it: a list of\n  { path, symbol?, line?, note? }. Resources point at CODE — NOT at other\n  pebbles the way references do.\n\nRules are NOT referenced by a finding.\n\n## Append-only & supersede\n\nFindings are an APPEND-ONLY evidence log — never update or delete one. A\nnew answer to the same question is a NEW finding that SUPERSEDES the old\n(a supersede link; the old file is untouched), and the chain's LATEST entry\nis the current truth. Deprecation exists but is exceptional — the norm is\nsupersession.\n\n## Quick map — component → what's under it\n\n| Component | Under it (shape / allowed values) |\n|---|---|\n| content.severity | critical · high · medium · low · info |\n| content.category | bug · verified-working · spec-violation · security · performance · regression · tech-debt · ux · data · other (+ research-log categories) |\n| content.state | open · resolved · info (disposition at birth; legacy: investigating / wont-fix / false-positive) |\n| status (pebble) | DERIVED from state — open→broken · resolved→verified · info→neutral |\n| content.body | markdown: ## Observation / Evidence / Impact / Root cause / Solution / Solution steps |\n| pebble.name | one-line finding title |\n| references | the pebble/unit the finding is about (model field, node, api, code, …) — not rules/skills/knowledge/findings |\n| resources | code links → { path, symbol?, line?, note? } to the file(s) the finding is about |\n\n## Finding CLI — all finding operations live here\n\nThe primary way to file a finding is `add-finding`, which creates a dated\nreport under `findings/` and cross-links it BOTH ways to its target (pebble\nAND unit). The write is HARDENED: severity and state are closed\nvocabularies, and category + body are required.\n\nFILE a problem — the `--body` is FULL markdown that FOLLOWS the default\nskeleton above. Deliver the multiline body with a heredoc (markdown stays\nliteral, no escaping) — a single-quoted `<<'MD'` heredoc is safest; the\n`$'## Observation\\n\\n…'` ANSI-C form works too but you must escape every\nnewline. Keep the six headers verbatim and in order; append bullets under\nEvidence and numbered steps under Solution steps:\n\n    pebbles-cli add-finding models/service-request.pebble --unit <fieldId> --title \"customer_id missing FK constraint\" --category spec-violation --severity high --body \"$(cat <<'MD'\n    ## Observation\n\n    service_requests.customer_id has no foreign-key constraint, so a deleted customer can orphan rows.\n\n    ## Evidence\n\n    - database/migrations/2026_..._create_service_requests.php L18 — column added, no ->foreign()\n    - No FK shown by SHOW CREATE TABLE service_requests\n\n    ## Impact\n\n    Orphaned service requests after a customer delete; joins silently drop rows; reporting undercounts.\n\n    ## Root cause\n\n    The migration declared the column but never the constraint.\n\n    ## Solution\n\n    Add the foreign key with an ON DELETE rule in a new migration.\n\n    ## Solution steps\n\n    1. New migration: table->foreign('customer_id')->references('id')->on('customers')->cascadeOnDelete().\n    2. Orphan-check + backfill existing rows before enabling the constraint.\n    MD\n    )\"\n\nThe whole body is ONE --body argument; every other flag stays before it. To\nadd more evidence later you do NOT edit this finding — you SUPERSEDE it with\na new one (below), re-sending the full body.\n\nFILE a pass proof (green evidence — disposition resolves from the category):\n\n    pebbles-cli add-finding models/user.pebble --title \"email uniqueness verified\" --category verified-working --body \"users.email has a unique index (migration L42) and UserTest::test_unique_email passes\"\n\nSUPERSEDE an earlier finding (append-only — the old file is untouched):\n\n    pebbles-cli add-finding models/user.pebble --title \"re-checked: still open\" --category bug --supersedes findings/2026-...-email.pebble --body \"...\"\n\nFlags: --title · --category · --severity (critical|high|medium|low|info) ·\n--disposition (open|resolved|info, else derived from category) · --unit\n<id> · --body <markdown> · --supersedes <finding-path>.\n\nINSPECT / read / validate (there is no edit — findings are append-only):\n\n    pebbles-cli refs findings/<file>.pebble\n    pebbles-cli get findings/<file>.pebble\n    pebbles-cli validate findings/<file>.pebble\n\nRESOURCES — link the file(s) the finding is about (matches its Evidence):\n\n    pebbles-cli link-code findings/<file>.pebble --file database/migrations/..._create_service_requests.php --line 18\n\nA finding can also be created with the generic `create findings --content`\n(same validated shape: severity, category, state, body) when not attaching\nto a target — but `add-finding` is preferred because it wires the target\nlinks for you.\n\n## Method lives elsewhere\n\nThis doc DEFINES the components. HOW to hunt for findings, gather evidence,\nand record pass/fail proofs lives in the **extraction-by-kind** and\n**verification-model** docs, which reference the categories above.",
